  "tutorialTempoTitle": "Tempo, beats, and the pendulum",
  "tutorialTempoBody": "The pendulum swings once per beat, and the big number is the tempo in BPM (beats per minute) — how many beats fit into one minute. The row of dots shows where you are inside the bar: the brighter first dot is the accented downbeat.",
  "tutorialTempoExample": "60 BPM = 1 beat per second\n120 BPM = 2 beats per second (twice as fast)",
  "tutorialBpmDragTitle": "Set your own tempo",
  "tutorialBpmDragBody": "Slow practice is the secret to clean playing: pick a tempo where you can play every note correctly, and only raise it once it feels easy. The slider goes from 30 (very slow) to 240 (very fast).",
  "tutorialBpmDragAction": "drag the tempo slider to any value.",
  "tutorialSequenceTitle": "Your note pattern",
  "tutorialSequenceBody": "This metronome does more than click — it can play a melody, one note per beat, looping your pattern while you play along. This panel shows the pattern loaded right now. Tap it any time to edit the notes without leaving this page.",
  "tutorialToggleTitle": "Click, notes, or both",
  "tutorialToggleBody": "\"Click\" is the classic tick that keeps time. \"Sound\" plays your note pattern on the selected instrument. Keep both on to hear the melody riding on top of the beat, or turn one off to focus.",
  "tutorialToggleAction": "tap one of the switches off, then on again.",
  "tutorialMeterTitle": "Meter and subdivision",
  "tutorialMeterBody": "The time signature groups beats into bars: in 4/4 you count 1-2-3-4 and start over, and beat 1 gets the accent. The beat unit subdivides each beat into smaller clicks, which helps when your notes move faster than the beat.",
  "tutorialMeterExample": "4/4 = 4 beats per bar (most common)\n3/4 = counts in 3, like a waltz\nEighth beat unit = 2 clicks per beat",
  "tutorialTransportTitle": "Hear it live",
  "tutorialTransportBody": "Everything is set — press Start and listen: the accented first beat, then your notes landing on each beat. Stop pauses the session; Reset jumps back to the beginning of your pattern.",
  "tutorialTransportAction": "press Start and listen for a bar or two.",
  "tutorialAdvancedTitle": "Advanced settings",
  "tutorialAdvancedBody": "When the defaults feel limiting, open this panel to change the click sound, choose the instrument that plays your notes, adjust accents, or shift the base octave up and down.",
  "tutorialHomePracticeTitle": "Welcome! Practice starts here",
  "tutorialHomePracticeBody": "Metrinote is a metronome that can also play the notes you want to practice, so you hear the beat and the melody together. This button opens the practice page with your current note pattern already loaded.",
  "tutorialHomeHistoryTitle": "Your practice history",
  "tutorialHomeHistoryBody": "Every session is tracked here: minutes practiced over the last 7 days, your most used tempo, and your favorite instrument. Set a daily goal and the progress ring keeps you honest.",
  "tutorialHomeTabsTitle": "Four tabs, one workflow",
  "tutorialHomeTabsBody": "Practice is home base. Sequences is where you build and save note patterns. Tools generates patterns for you. Basics explains the music terms this app uses. Let's visit them in order.",
  "tutorialHomeExamplesTitle": "Start from an example",
  "tutorialHomeExamplesBody": "Not sure what to practice? These ready-made patterns load with one tap — a Western major scale or an Eastern raga cycle. Load one, then tweak it to make it yours.",
  "tutorialHomeSequencesTitle": "Write your own pattern",
  "tutorialHomeSequencesBody": "Type note names separated by spaces, or tap the note chips below the field. Both Western letters (A B C…) and Eastern sargam (S R G M…) work. Give the pattern a name and save it to reuse any time.",
  "tutorialHomeSequencesExample": "C D E F → four notes, one per beat\nG - → '-' holds G for an extra beat\nE/F → '/' fits two notes into one beat\nC' high octave · C, low octave",
  "tutorialHomeToolsTitle": "Let Tools do the typing",
  "tutorialHomeToolsBody": "Tools has two generators that write patterns for you: a scale builder and a jianpu (numbered notation) converter. Let's take a quick look at both.",
  "tutorialHomeScaleGenTitle": "Scale pattern generator",
  "tutorialHomeScaleGenBody": "Pick a root key, scale type, octave range, and direction — it writes the full pattern for you. \"Use pattern\" drops the result straight into your sequence editor.",
  "tutorialHomeJianpuTitle": "Jianpu converter",
  "tutorialHomeJianpuBody": "If you read numbered notation (1 2 3 = do re mi), paste it here and it becomes a playable pattern. Octave dots and dashes for held notes are understood too.",
  "tutorialHomeBasicsTitle": "Learn the words",
  "tutorialHomeBasicsBody": "One more stop: Basics is a plain-language glossary of every term this app uses. Let's read the four most important ones together.",
  "tutorialBasicsBpmBody": "BPM means beats per minute: 60 BPM is exactly one beat every second. This is the number you set with the tempo slider on the practice page. Golden rule: start slower than feels comfortable.",
  "tutorialBasicsMeterBody": "The top number says how many beats each bar contains, and beat 1 always gets the accent. You choose this in the meter chip on the practice page — 4/4 is the safe default for most music.",
  "tutorialBasicsSubdivisionBody": "Subdivision splits each beat into smaller equal clicks: eighths give 2 clicks per beat, sixteenths give 4. Turn it on when your notes move faster than the main beat.",
  "tutorialBasicsNotationBody": "This app accepts two naming systems for the same notes: Western letters (C D E F G A B) and Eastern sargam (S R G M P D N). The nearby cards explain octave marks, held notes, and grouping.",
  "tutorialHomeReturnTitle": "Back to home base",
  "tutorialHomeReturnBody": "That's the tour of the tabs. Select Practice yourself so you always know how to get back to your starting point.",
  "tutorialStartSessionTitle": "Start when you are ready",
  "tutorialStartSessionBody": "Now press Start Metronome yourself. That opens the practice workspace, where the hands-on metronome tutorial will continue.",
  "tutorialHomeSettingsTitle": "Settings and replay",
  "tutorialHomeSettingsBody": "Theme, colors, and language live behind this gear. If you ever forget how something works, open Settings and tap \"Watch tutorial again\". Press Done, then we'll return to Practice together.",
  "tutorialScoreTitle": "Score practice on iPad",
  "tutorialScoreBody": "On larger screens in landscape, load a sheet-music image or PDF here and practice while the metronome stays visible beside it. You can zoom, flip pages, and go fullscreen.",
